Methodology 1 — Reader-first investigation

This has been one of the most successful proposals.

The idea is to establish the actual reader mechanics first, then use those mechanics to identify the data structures it consumes.

Why it works:

    Dimensions are known.
    Storage widths are known.
    Axis contracts are known.
    Query inputs and interpolation mechanics can be checked independently.
    Candidate data can be assessed against an actual execution mechanism. 

Decision: retain as the default starting point for every new calibration investigation.

Methodology 2 — Permissive structural discovery

This was an important correction to the earlier overly restrictive verification approach.

Instead of requiring a complete binary-to-reader proof before examining anything, generate plausible candidates using:

    Array dimensions.
    Monotonicity.
    Value spacing.
    Axis-pair adjacency.
    Repeated payload patterns.
    Similarity to known EL19A structures. 

E0BE/E0CE demonstrates both the strength and limitation of this approach. It is a legitimate L2 discovery candidate, but the pointer audit did not connect it to 9B2E.

Decision: retain this method, but use it to generate and rank candidates—not establish their identity.

Methodology 3 — The optimized discovery funnel

WO64424 proposed the strongest end-to-end workflow:

    Verify source identity and available resources.
    Reuse established reader patterns and caller inventories.
    Generate structural candidate arrays and maps.
    Filter by dimension, storage type, axes and value patterns.
    Establish direct or indirect pointer references.
    Check runtime query compatibility and actual reader arguments.
    Trace ROM/RAM initialization and copy paths.
    Trace producers, consumers and update conditions.
    Assign evidence grades and provisional or confirmed names. 

Assessment: This is the best methodology for the current phase. It balances broad discovery with strict confirmation.

Methodology 4 — GBR and task-context reconstruction

The global LDC inventory, dispatcher ancestry, task-frame audit and save/restore investigations were technically reasonable.

They also produced one useful result: the separate sub_AE8CE context with GBR=0xF49E was identified and correctly kept separate from the 0x473xx family.

But after WO64414 through WO64422, the repeated attempt to resolve the root GBR for 9B2E has had diminishing returns.

The most recent findings still show:

    STC GBR at 0x475B4 and 0x4760C.
    No proven root initialization or context handoff.
    No connection to the F49E context. 

Decision: stop repeated root-GBR investigations. Reopen only if a new pointer descriptor, context block, data-reference path or independent execution trace appears.

Methodology 5 — Producer, interrupt and data-freshness tracing

This has been particularly effective.

The work on FFFF84BF, FFFF8496, FFFF84A4, FFFF849A and the FFFF859C copy path demonstrates why knowing the data structure is not enough.

It is necessary to distinguish:

    Where a value is calculated.
    Where it is copied.
    Which interrupt updates it.
    Whether another path overwrites it.
    Whether the reader sees a current value or an older state. 

Decision: increase its priority. It has produced more actionable progress than the repeated GBR ancestry work.

